7
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:51.638
<v Marcus>Wait — before FormEBM-v3 pre-filtered candidate beams, didn't Wave Four's unnormalized energy scorer stumble on big multi-line edits?

8
00:01:51.908 --> 00:02:15.896
<v Rohan>It did! Unnormalized scoring penalized valid large edits, dropping out-of-distribution H15 AUROC to zero point two five eight five. Our span-normalized two-head FormEBM-v3 — just six point zero five million parameters — separates defect density from edit depth, hitting zero point nine four three seven unseen-shape AUROC and resolving that edit-size inversion!

9
00:02:16.166 --> 00:02:26.491
<v Mateo>And when a genuinely novel task misses the cheap gears and wakes Qwen 3.6 35B-A3B or Nemotron 120B locally?

10
00:02:26.761 --> 00:02:46.765
<v Rohan>Custom Apple Metal and Rust Burn CubeCL four-bit shaders stream active experts straight off NVMe in nine hundred seventy megabytes of RAM — one point four seven percent of FP16 — with one hundred percent exact token parity, lifting 120B verified pass rates from forty-three point seven five to sixty-two point five percent!

11
00:02:46.955 --> 00:02:52.500
<v Marcus>Give us the scoreboard across every coding suite — how many broken commits slipped past the verifier?

12
00:02:52.770 --> 00:03:15.677
<v Rohan>Zero point zero zero percent broken commits! We hit ninety-one point six seven percent on our forty-eight-task suite, ninety point six two percent on BigCodeBench v0.1.3, forty out of forty on H15-Iterative with Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5, and replayed crystallized fixes in zero point three two five microseconds at one point seven seven microjoules!

13
00:03:15.867 --> 00:03:20.152
<v Marcus>And keep us honest: which repair rung failed its own promotion ceiling?

14
00:03:20.422 --> 00:03:39.624
<v Rohan>On our hundred-fifty-one-task rce repair audit, Rung F hit two point five percent above our two point zero percent ceiling — so holds returned false and Rung F stayed locked in observe mode, just like Foundry pilot TF-2026-06's neural router got crushed sixty-three times by an integer grid!

15
00:03:39.894 --> 00:03:52.027
<v Mateo>Route cheap, verify with integer totality, and crystallize novelty downward! Explore synthesis.openie.dev, and join Priya, Sean, and Claire next at the OpenIE Knowledge Desk!
